  <h2 class="kd-h2" id="who-was-who">Who was who?</h2>
  <p class="kd-sub">In many places long ago, the family you were born into decided a lot about your life: the work you did, who you could marry, even whether you were free. Climb down these ladders from the ruler at the top to the people at the bottom, then <a href="/societies/#born">be born into history</a> and see where chance puts you.</p>
  <div class="kc-ladders">
  <!-- build:kids-ladders -->
<article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Ancient Egypt</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hatshepsut.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>The pharaoh</b> The pharaoh was Egypt’s ruler, who led the army, made the laws and was honoured almost like a god.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span><b>High officials</b> Officials such as the vizier ran Egypt for the pharaoh, keeping its records and judging its court cases.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/herihor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Priests</b> Priests looked after the temples, where they washed and dressed the gods’ statues and gave them food every day.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/egyptian-hieroglyphs.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Scribes</b> Scribes kept Egypt’s records, writing down harvests, taxes and court cases on rolls of papyrus.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/battle-of-kadesh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Soldiers</b> Egyptian soldiers fought on foot or from fast, two-wheeled chariots pulled by horses.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">6</span><span><b>Artisans</b> Skilled craftsmen built and painted the royal tombs, and they went on strike when their food did not arrive.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">7</span><span><b>Farmers</b> Most Egyptians were farmers who grew wheat, barley and flax in fields watered by the Nile’s yearly flood.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">8</span><span><b>Enslaved people</b> Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; many were prisoners taken in war.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/athens">Classical Athens</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pericles.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Richest citizens</b> The richest Athenians were expected to pay for warships and for the plays at the city’s festivals.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span><b>Knights</b> Knights were rich Athenians who could afford a horse and rode in the city’s cavalry.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/socrates.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Hoplite class</b> Farmers of the hoplite class bought their own shields and spears and fought side by side.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">4</span><span><b>Poorer citizens</b> Poorer citizen men could vote in the Assembly, serve on juries and row the city’s warships.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aristotle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Metics</b> Metics were free people from other places who lived and worked in Athens but could not vote.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">6</span><span><b>Enslaved people</b> Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; some were later freed.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/ancient-rome">Ancient Rome</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/trajan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>The emperor</b> The emperor commanded the army, made the laws and had his face on the coins.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/roman-senate.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Senators</b> Senators were rich landowners who advised the emperor and governed the provinces.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pliny-the-elder.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Equestrians</b> Equestrians, or knights, were wealthy men who ran the emperor’s offices, money and some of his armies.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">4</span><span><b>Town councillors</b> Every Roman town had a council of rich local men who ran its markets, temples and festivals.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/auxilia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Soldiers</b> Roman soldiers served for about 25 years and were given land or money when they retired.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/latifundium.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Free people</b> Most Romans were farmers, craftspeople and shopkeepers who worked hard to feed their families.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">7</span><span><b>Freed slaves</b> Enslaved people who were set free became Roman citizens, and their children were born free.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">8</span><span><b>Enslaved people</b> Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; some were later set free.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/vikings">the Vikings</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/harald-bluetooth.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Kings</b> Viking kings led armies and fleets, gave gifts to their warriors and in time made their people Christian.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span><b>Jarls</b> Jarls were powerful lords, one step below the king, who owned great farms and ruled whole regions.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">3</span><span><b>Chieftains</b> Chieftains led the farmers of their district in war and at the assemblies where laws were made.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/varangian-guard.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Retainers</b> Kings and chieftains kept bands of warriors who lived with them and were rewarded with gifts.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">5</span><span><b>Free farmers</b> Most Vikings were farmers, not raiders: they grew grain, kept animals and met at assemblies to make laws.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">6</span><span><b>Freed slaves</b> Some thralls were set free, and some were even given land to farm by their former owners.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">7</span><span><b>Thralls</b> Thralls were enslaved people, often captured in raids, who were owned by others and made to work without pay.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Feudal Europe</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/henry-ii-of-england.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>The king</b> Kings were crowned in church and ruled with the help of great lords, who promised to be loyal to them.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/henry-the-lion.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Great lords</b> Great lords such as dukes and counts lived in castles, ruled large lands and led knights for the king.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/wolfram-von-eschenbach.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Knights</b> Knights were warriors who fought on horseback and were given land in return for serving their lord.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/albertus-magnus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Priests, monks and nuns</b> Priests looked after village churches, while monks and nuns lived together in monasteries, praying and working.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">5</span><span><b>Townspeople</b> Townspeople were merchants and craftspeople, and many towns won the right to run their own markets and councils.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/serfdom.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Serfs and villeins</b> Serfs were farmers who had to work on their lord’s land and could not move away without his permission.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">7</span><span><b>Enslaved people</b> Enslaved people were owned by others and worked without pay; in England slavery had died out by about 1150.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/han-china">Han China</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/liu-bang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>The emperor</b> The Han emperor was called the Son of Heaven, and people were not allowed to say his name.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/han-xin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Kings and marquesses</b> Han kings were usually relatives of the emperor, and they lived on taxes from the lands they were given.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/xiao-he.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Officials and scholars</b> Many Han officials studied the books of Confucius and were chosen for being good sons and honest men.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yuan-shao.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Great landed families</b> Rich Han families owned large farms, and poorer families rented their fields and gave them about half the harvest.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">5</span><span><b>Farmers</b> Most people in Han China were farmers, and men had to work for the government for one month each year.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">6</span><span><b>Artisans</b> Han craftspeople made shiny lacquer cups, bronze mirrors and fine silk cloth.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">7</span><span><b>Merchants</b> Some Han merchants grew very rich, but laws kept many of them from wearing silk or working for the government.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">8</span><span><b>Enslaved people</b> Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; in Han China there were few of them.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/tokugawa-japan">Tokugawa Japan</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">1</span><span><b>The emperor and the court nobles</b> The emperor lived in Kyoto and was greatly honoured, but the shogun, not the emperor, ruled Japan.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tokugawa-ieyasu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>The shogun</b> The shogun was Japan’s real ruler, a general from the Tokugawa family who governed from the city of Edo.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/date-masamune.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Daimyo</b> Daimyo were great lords who ruled parts of Japan for the shogun and had to live in his city every other year.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bushido.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Samurai</b> Samurai were warriors who wore two swords, and in the long Edo peace most of them worked as officials and guards.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/buddhism.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Buddhist and Shinto clergy</b> Buddhist priests looked after temples and kept a record of every family that belonged to them.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ninomiya-sontoku.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Peasants</b> Most people in Edo Japan were farmers who grew rice and paid a large share of it to their lord.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">7</span><span><b>Kawata outcasts</b> Kawata families did jobs such as leatherwork that others thought unclean, and they were made to live apart.</span></li></ol></article><article class="kc-ladder"><h3 class="kc-ladder-t"><a href="/societies/aztec-empire">the Aztec Empire</a></h3><ol class="kc-ladder-l"><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/huey-tlatoani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>The huey tlatoani</b> The huey tlatoani, or “great speaker”, ruled the Aztecs from the island city of Tenochtitlan.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tlacaelel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Nobles</b> Aztec nobles were born into their rank, went to temple schools and became generals, officials and priests.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/templo-mayor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Priests</b> Aztec priests looked after the temples, taught in the schools and led the festivals for the gods.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pochteca.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Long-distance merchants and luxury craftsmen</b> Aztec merchants made long journeys to faraway lands to trade for colourful feathers and other treasures.</span></li><li><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/chinampa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><b>Commoners</b> Most Aztecs were farmers who grew maize, beans and squash and gave part of what they made to their rulers.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">6</span><span><b>Landless tenants</b> Some Aztec farmers had no land of their own and worked the fields of a noble instead.</span></li><li><span class="kc-rung" aria-hidden="true">7</span><span><b>Enslaved people</b> Enslaved people had to work for an owner without pay, but their children were born free.</span></li></ol></article>
