<section class="kd-sec" id="t-egypt" aria-labelledby="t-egypt-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-egypt-h">Ancient Egypt</h2><p class="kd-intro">Pharaohs, pyramids and the gift of the Nile.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-giza"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-giza.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="180" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Great Pyramid of Giza</span><span class="kd-card-d">Built about 4,500 years ago, it was the tallest building on Earth for some 3,800 years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/khufu"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khufu.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="519" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Khufu</span><span class="kd-card-d">The pharaoh who built the Great Pyramid; the only complete statue of him ever found is just 7.5 centimetres tall.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii.jpg" alt="" width="280" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ramesses II</span><span class="kd-card-d">He ruled for 66 years and put up giant statues of himself all over Egypt.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hatshepsut.jpg" alt="" width="315" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hatshepsut</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the few women to rule Egypt as pharaoh; some statues show her with a king's false beard.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akhenaten.jpg" alt="" width="315" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Akhenaten</span><span class="kd-card-d">A pharaoh who tried to make Egyptians worship a single sun god, the Aten.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii.jpg" alt="" width="317" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Cleopatra VII</span><span class="kd-card-d">Egypt's last queen spoke many languages and was the first of her family to learn Egyptian.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/nile"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nile.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="214" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Nile</span><span class="kd-card-d">Africa's longest river: its yearly flood left rich black mud for farming.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="374" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Rosetta Stone</span><span class="kd-card-d">The same message in three scripts helped scholars finally read hieroglyphs in 1822.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-simbel.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Abu Simbel</span><span class="kd-card-d">Two temples carved into a cliff, cut into blocks and moved uphill in the 1960s to save them from a new lake.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="235" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Deir el-Bahari</span><span class="kd-card-d">The terraced temple of Queen Hatshepsut, built against the cliffs near Luxor.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/rhind-mathematical-papyrus"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhind-mathematical-papyrus.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="192" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Rhind Mathematical Papyrus</span><span class="kd-card-d">An Egyptian maths book about 3,600 years old, full of puzzles about bread, beer and pyramids.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-egypt">Play the Ancient Egypt Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-greece" aria-labelledby="t-greece-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-greece-h">Ancient Greece</h2><p class="kd-intro">Thinkers, athletes and the first democracy.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parthenon.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="225" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Parthenon</span><span class="kd-card-d">A marble temple for the goddess Athena, built on a hill above Athens nearly 2,500 years ago.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/athens"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/athens.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="180" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Athens</span><span class="kd-card-d">The city where citizens first voted on their own laws, in a democracy.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonidas-i.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="464" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Leonidas I</span><span class="kd-card-d">A king of Sparta who led his 300 Spartans at the pass of Thermopylae in 480 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/homer"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/homer.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Homer</span><span class="kd-card-d">The poet said to have told the stories of the Trojan War and of Odysseus' long voyage home.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/socrates"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/socrates.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Socrates</span><span class="kd-card-d">He taught by asking questions, and he never wrote a single book.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/plato"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plato.jpg" alt="" width="280" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Plato</span><span class="kd-card-d">A pupil of Socrates who started one of the first schools, the Academy.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristotle.jpg" alt="" width="314" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Aristotle</span><span class="kd-card-d">He studied everything from animals and plants to poetry, and he taught Alexander the Great.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="" width="315" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Alexander the Great</span><span class="kd-card-d">By the age of 30 he ruled an empire that stretched from Greece to India.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ancient-olympic-games"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ancient-olympic-games.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="212" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ancient Olympic Games</span><span class="kd-card-d">Held at Olympia every four years for more than a thousand years, with races, wrestling and chariots.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/colossus-of-rhodes"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colossus-of-rhodes.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="437" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Colossus of Rhodes</span><span class="kd-card-d">A bronze statue of the sun god about as tall as a ten-storey building; an earthquake toppled it.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/lighthouse-of-alexandria"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lighthouse-of-alexandria.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="272" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Lighthouse of Alexandria</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the Seven Wonders, its fire guided ships into harbour for many centuries.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-greece">Play the Ancient Greece Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-rome" aria-labelledby="t-rome-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-rome-h">Ancient Rome</h2><p class="kd-intro">Emperors, legions and roads that led everywhere.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="" width="309" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Julius Caesar</span><span class="kd-card-d">A famous general who became master of Rome; the month of July is named after him.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/cicero"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cicero.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="480" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Cicero</span><span class="kd-card-d">Rome's greatest speaker; hundreds of his letters still survive.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hannibal.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="387" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hannibal</span><span class="kd-card-d">Rome's great enemy, who marched an army with elephants across the Alps.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/roman-forum"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-forum.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="131" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Roman Forum</span><span class="kd-card-d">The busy heart of the city, full of temples, law courts and shops.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/appian-way"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/appian-way.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Appian Way</span><span class="kd-card-d">A stone road begun in 312 BCE; you can still walk along parts of it today.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/trajan"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trajan.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="417" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Trajan</span><span class="kd-card-d">Under this emperor the Roman Empire reached its largest size.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Marcus Aurelius</span><span class="kd-card-d">An emperor who wrote his thoughts in a notebook we still read, the Meditations.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/nero"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nero.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="435" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Nero</span><span class="kd-card-d">An emperor who loved music and theatre, and even sang on stage himself.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pompeii.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="197" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Pompeii</span><span class="kd-card-d">A town buried by the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 CE, so well preserved that you can walk its streets.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantine-i.jpg" alt="" width="293" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Constantine I</span><span class="kd-card-d">The emperor who founded Constantinople, the city we now call Istanbul.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-rome">Play the Ancient Rome Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-knights" aria-labelledby="t-knights-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-knights-h">Knights and Castles</h2><p class="kd-intro">Kings, queens and castles of the Middle Ages.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="533" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">William the Conqueror</span><span class="kd-card-d">He sailed from Normandy and won the Battle of Hastings in 1066.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux-tapestry"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayeux-tapestry.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="232" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Bayeux Tapestry</span><span class="kd-card-d">A 70-metre-long embroidery that tells the story of 1066 like a comic strip.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="180" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Eleanor of Aquitaine</span><span class="kd-card-d">She was queen of France and then queen of England, and the mother of two English kings.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="180" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Richard I of England</span><span class="kd-card-d">Called the Lionheart, he spent only a few months of his reign in England.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/knights-templar.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="192" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Knights Templar</span><span class="kd-card-d">An order of knights who were also monks, dressed in white with a red cross.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/krak-des-chevaliers"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krak-des-chevaliers.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="214" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Krak des Chevaliers</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the best-preserved castles of the Crusades, high on a hill in Syria.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="477" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Joan of Arc</span><span class="kd-card-d">A farm girl from France who led an army when she was about 17.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/magna-carta.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Magna Carta</span><span class="kd-card-d">A charter of 1215 which said that even the king had to obey the law.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-knights">Play the Knights and Castles Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-vikings" aria-labelledby="t-vikings-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-vikings-h">Vikings</h2><p class="kd-intro">Sailors, traders and explorers from the far north.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/leif-erikson"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leif-erikson.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="480" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Leif Erikson</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Viking explorer who reached North America about 500 years before Columbus.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/erik-the-red"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erik-the-red.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="453" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Erik the Red</span><span class="kd-card-d">He named his icy new home Greenland to make settlers want to go there.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/thor"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thor.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="425" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Thor</span><span class="kd-card-d">The Norse god of thunder; Thursday is named after him.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="230" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Oseberg ship</span><span class="kd-card-d">A beautifully carved Viking ship found buried in a grave mound in Norway.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/gokstad-ship"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gokstad-ship.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Gokstad ship</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Viking ship so well made that a copy sailed across the Atlantic in 1893.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">L'Anse aux Meadows</span><span class="kd-card-d">The remains of a Viking settlement in Canada, about a thousand years old.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/froso-runestone"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/froso-runestone.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="651" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Frösö Runestone</span><span class="kd-card-d">A stone carved with runes, the letters the Vikings wrote with.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lindisfarne.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Lindisfarne</span><span class="kd-card-d">An island monastery in England whose raid by Vikings in 793 shocked Europe.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-vikings">Play the Vikings Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-explorers" aria-labelledby="t-explorers-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-explorers-h">Great Explorers</h2><p class="kd-intro">Brave travellers who crossed oceans, deserts and mountains.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xuanzang.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="716" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Xuanzang</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Chinese monk who walked all the way to India and back to collect books.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marco-polo.jpg" alt="" width="245" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Marco Polo</span><span class="kd-card-d">A merchant from Venice who travelled to China and wrote a famous book about it.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta.jpg" alt="" width="291" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ibn Battuta</span><span class="kd-card-d">He travelled for nearly 30 years across Africa and Asia, further than anyone else of his time.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zheng-he.jpg" alt="" width="280" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Zheng He</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Chinese admiral who led huge fleets across the Indian Ocean; his treasure ships are said to be among the largest wooden ships ever built.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="409" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Prince Henry the Navigator</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Portuguese prince who sent ships down the coast of Africa, though he rarely sailed himself.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="387" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Christopher Columbus</span><span class="kd-card-d">In 1492 he sailed across the Atlantic, hoping to reach Asia.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama.jpg" alt="" width="309" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Vasco da Gama</span><span class="kd-card-d">The first European to sail all the way from Europe to India, in 1498.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="395" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ferdinand Magellan</span><span class="kd-card-d">His fleet was the first to sail around the whole world, though he died on the way.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-cook.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="401" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">James Cook</span><span class="kd-card-d">A British captain who mapped the coasts of New Zealand and eastern Australia.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-explorers">Play the Great Explorers Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-inventions" aria-labelledby="t-inventions-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-inventions-h">Inventions and Ideas</h2><p class="kd-intro">Clever ideas that changed how people live.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi.jpg" alt="" width="255" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Code of Hammurabi</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the oldest written law codes, carved on a tall black stone nearly 3,800 years ago.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="325" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Library of Alexandria</span><span class="kd-card-d">An ancient library that tried to collect a copy of every book it could find.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/archimedes"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/archimedes.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Archimedes</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Greek inventor who is said to have shouted &quot;Eureka!&quot; in his bath.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/euclid"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euclid.jpg" alt="" width="311" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Euclid</span><span class="kd-card-d">His geometry book was used in schools for more than 2,000 years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/cai-lun"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cai-lun.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="400" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Cai Lun</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Chinese court official remembered for improving paper-making around the year 105.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi.jpg" alt="" width="315" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">al-Khwarizmi</span><span class="kd-card-d">A scholar in Baghdad whose name gave us the word &quot;algorithm&quot;.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="325" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">House of Wisdom</span><span class="kd-card-d">A great library and meeting place for scholars in Baghdad.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg.jpg" alt="" width="315" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Johannes Gutenberg</span><span class="kd-card-d">His printing press with movable metal letters made books far cheaper.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="459" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Leonardo da Vinci</span><span class="kd-card-d">A painter and inventor who filled notebooks with designs for flying machines.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="406" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Galileo Galilei</span><span class="kd-card-d">In 1610 he looked through his telescope and discovered four moons of Jupiter.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isaac-newton.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="385" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Isaac Newton</span><span class="kd-card-d">He explained gravity, the force that makes apples fall and keeps the Moon in orbit.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-inventions">Play the Inventions and Ideas Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-buildings" aria-labelledby="t-buildings-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-buildings-h">Amazing Buildings</h2><p class="kd-intro">Wonders of stone, brick and marble from around the world.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="212" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Great Wall of China</span><span class="kd-card-d">Built and rebuilt over 2,000 years, its walls run for thousands of kilometres.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taj-mahal.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="211" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Taj Mahal</span><span class="kd-card-d">A white marble tomb built by the emperor Shah Jahan for his wife, Mumtaz Mahal.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/angkor-wat.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="218" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Angkor Wat</span><span class="kd-card-d">A vast temple in Cambodia, often called the largest religious monument in the world.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/borobudur.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Borobudur</span><span class="kd-card-d">A giant Buddhist monument on the island of Java with more than 500 Buddha statues.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="193" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hagia Sophia</span><span class="kd-card-d">For nearly a thousand years it was the largest church in the world.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alhambra.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Alhambra</span><span class="kd-card-d">A palace in Granada famous for its gardens, fountains and patterned walls.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/machu-picchu.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Machu Picchu</span><span class="kd-card-d">An Inca city high in the Andes mountains, built around 1450.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/petra"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petra.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Petra</span><span class="kd-card-d">A city carved into pink sandstone cliffs in Jordan.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-sun"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-sun.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="180" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Pyramid of the Sun</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the largest pyramids in the world, in the ancient city of Teotihuacan in Mexico.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat-of-ur"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ziggurat-of-ur.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="140" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ziggurat of Ur</span><span class="kd-card-d">A stepped temple tower built about 4,000 years ago in what is now Iraq.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-minar"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qutb-minar.jpg" alt="" width="278" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Qutb Minar</span><span class="kd-card-d">A red sandstone tower in Delhi about 73 metres tall, begun around the year 1200.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hanging-gardens-of-babylon"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hanging-gardens-of-babylon.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="221" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hanging Gardens of Babylon</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the Seven Wonders; no one has ever found it, and some think it was only a legend.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-buildings">Play the Amazing Buildings Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-china" aria-labelledby="t-china-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-china-h">Ancient China</h2><p class="kd-intro">Emperors, inventors and a wall across the mountains.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="212" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Great Wall of China</span><span class="kd-card-d">Guards in its watchtowers sent smoke and fire signals to warn that raiders were coming.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="431" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Qin Shi Huang</span><span class="kd-card-d">China's first emperor made everyone use the same coins, the same weights and the same writing.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/confucius"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/confucius.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="299" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Confucius</span><span class="kd-card-d">A teacher who lived about 2,500 years ago and taught people to be kind, honest and fair.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yangtze-river.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="212" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Yangtze River</span><span class="kd-card-d">Asia's longest river, about 6,300 kilometres long, flowing from the mountains of Tibet to the sea.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yellow-river.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="198" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Yellow River</span><span class="kd-card-d">Its water carries so much yellow mud that the river is named after its colour.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/changan"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/changan.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="372" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Chang'an</span><span class="kd-card-d">About 1,300 years ago it was one of the biggest cities in the world, with streets laid out in a neat grid.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luoyang.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Luoyang</span><span class="kd-card-d">Near this old capital, people carved tens of thousands of Buddha statues into the cliffs of the Longmen Grottoes.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dunhuang.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Dunhuang</span><span class="kd-card-d">A desert oasis on the Silk Road with hundreds of caves full of colourful paintings.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zheng-he.jpg" alt="" width="280" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Zheng He</span><span class="kd-card-d">An admiral who sailed seven times with giant treasure ships, reaching as far as East Africa.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kublai-khan.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="399" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Kublai Khan</span><span class="kd-card-d">The grandson of Genghis Khan, he became emperor of China and built a new capital where Beijing stands today.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-china">Play the Ancient China Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-africa" aria-labelledby="t-africa-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-africa-h">Africa's Great Kingdoms</h2><p class="kd-intro">Golden empires, trading cities and builders in stone.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mansa-musa.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="365" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Mansa Musa</span><span class="kd-card-d">The ruler of Mali whose journey to Mecca in 1324 carried so much gold that people talked about it for centuries.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timbuktu.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="220" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Timbuktu</span><span class="kd-card-d">A city at the edge of the Sahara where scholars wrote and copied thousands of books.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Great Zimbabwe</span><span class="kd-card-d">Its great curving walls were built from blocks of granite without any mortar.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes.jpg" alt="" width="302" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Benin Bronzes</span><span class="kd-card-d">Artists of the Kingdom of Benin made hundreds of brass plaques and heads for the royal palace.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lalibela.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="277" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Lalibela</span><span class="kd-card-d">Eleven churches here were carved down into solid rock about 800 years ago.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/meroe"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meroe.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="154" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Meroë</span><span class="kd-card-d">The royal cemeteries of this city of the Kingdom of Kush hold around 200 small, steep pyramids.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/kilwa-kisiwani"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kilwa-kisiwani.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Kilwa Kisiwani</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Swahili island town whose sultans grew rich trading gold from inland Africa.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="427" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Djinguereber Mosque</span><span class="kd-card-d">A mosque of mud brick in Timbuktu, built around 1327, that is given a fresh coat of mud every year.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/njinga-of-ndongo-and-matamba"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/njinga-of-ndongo-and-matamba.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="426" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Njinga of Ndongo and Matamba</span><span class="kd-card-d">Queen Njinga ruled in Angola for decades and fought hard to keep her lands free of Portuguese rule.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-africa">Play the Africa's Great Kingdoms Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-americas" aria-labelledby="t-americas-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-americas-h">Aztecs, Maya and Inca</h2><p class="kd-intro">Cities of the sun, the jaguar and the high Andes.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/machu-picchu.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Machu Picchu</span><span class="kd-card-d">Built for an Inca emperor around 1450 on a mountain ridge about 2,400 metres high.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="176" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Sacsayhuamán</span><span class="kd-card-d">On a hill above the Inca capital of Cusco, huge stone blocks were cut to fit so tightly that a knife blade cannot slip between them.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pachacuti.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="349" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Pachacuti</span><span class="kd-card-d">The Inca ruler who began turning a small kingdom into the largest empire in the Americas.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/templo-mayor.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Templo Mayor</span><span class="kd-card-d">The Aztecs' great temple had two shrines on top, one for the god of rain and one for the god of war.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/kinich-janaab-pakal"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kinich-janaab-pakal.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="412" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">K'inich Janaab Pakal</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Maya king of Palenque who was buried with a mask made of green jade.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atahualpa.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="413" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Atahualpa</span><span class="kd-card-d">The last ruler of the Inca Empire, captured by the Spanish in 1532.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/nazca-lines"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nazca-lines.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Nazca Lines</span><span class="kd-card-d">Giant drawings of animals scratched into the desert of Peru, best seen from the air.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii.jpg" alt="" width="261" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Moctezuma II</span><span class="kd-card-d">The Aztec emperor who met Hernán Cortés in 1519.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-americas">Play the Aztecs, Maya and Inca Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-pacific" aria-labelledby="t-pacific-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-pacific-h">Islands and Ocean Voyagers</h2><p class="kd-intro">Star-guided canoes, sacred kings and stone giants of the Pacific.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/easter-island.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Easter Island</span><span class="kd-card-d">Its people carved nearly 900 giant stone statues called moai.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="456" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Kamehameha I</span><span class="kd-card-d">A warrior king who united all the islands of Hawaii by 1810.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polynesians.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Polynesians</span><span class="kd-card-d">Polynesian sailors crossed thousands of kilometres of open ocean, steering by the stars, the waves and the birds.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/taputapuatea"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taputapuatea.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="161" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Taputapuatea</span><span class="kd-card-d">A great stone temple in French Polynesia where voyagers from many islands once gathered.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/haamonga-a-maui"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haamonga-a-maui.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Haʻamonga ʻa Maui</span><span class="kd-card-d">A stone gateway in Tonga made of three huge blocks of coral rock.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/samoan-islands"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samoan-islands.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="217" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Samoan Islands</span><span class="kd-card-d">Samoans are famous for their fine woven mats, their songs and their councils of chiefs.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-cook.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="401" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">James Cook</span><span class="kd-card-d">A British explorer who sailed the Pacific three times and drew maps of many of its islands.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/native-hawaiians"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/native-hawaiians.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Native Hawaiians</span><span class="kd-card-d">Hula, the Hawaiian dance, tells stories with movements of the hands, the hips and the feet.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/nukualofa"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nukualofa.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Nukuʻalofa</span><span class="kd-card-d">Tonga's capital, where the king's white wooden palace has stood by the sea since 1867.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-pacific">Play the Islands and Ocean Voyagers Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-india" aria-labelledby="t-india-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-india-h">India's Golden Ages</h2><p class="kd-intro">Emperors, mathematicians and monuments of the Indian subcontinent.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashoka.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="305" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ashoka</span><span class="kd-card-d">An emperor who gave up war and had messages of kindness carved on rocks and pillars across India.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/akbar"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akbar.jpg" alt="" width="311" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Akbar</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Mughal emperor who loved to hear people of different religions talk about their beliefs.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nalanda.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="202" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Nalanda</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the world's first great universities, where students came from as far away as China and Korea.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ajanta-caves"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ajanta-caves.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ajanta Caves</span><span class="kd-card-d">Caves carved into a cliff and painted with stories of the Buddha more than 1,500 years ago.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/aryabhata"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aryabhata.jpg" alt="" width="293" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Aryabhata</span><span class="kd-card-d">A mathematician who worked out more than 1,500 years ago that the Earth spins on its axis.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalidasa.jpg" alt="" width="293" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Kalidasa</span><span class="kd-card-d">India's most famous classical poet, who wrote plays and poems in Sanskrit.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/delhi"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/delhi.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="380" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Delhi</span><span class="kd-card-d">A city that has been a capital again and again for more than 800 years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="404" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Chandragupta Maurya</span><span class="kd-card-d">He founded the Maurya Empire, the first empire to rule most of India.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-jahan.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="463" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Shah Jahan</span><span class="kd-card-d">The Mughal emperor who built the Taj Mahal in memory of his wife, Mumtaz Mahal.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i.jpg" alt="" width="303" height="353" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Rajendra Chola I</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Chola king whose fleet sailed across the sea to Southeast Asia.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-india">Play the India's Golden Ages Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-islamic" aria-labelledby="t-islamic-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-islamic-h">The Islamic Golden Age</h2><p class="kd-intro">Scholars, poets and travellers of a world stretching from Spain to Central Asia.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/samarra"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samarra.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Samarra</span><span class="kd-card-d">An Abbasid capital on the River Tigris, famous for the great spiral minaret of its mosque, built in the 800s.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="325" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">House of Wisdom</span><span class="kd-card-d">A great library in Baghdad where scholars translated books from Greek, Persian and Sanskrit into Arabic.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alhambra.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Alhambra</span><span class="kd-card-d">Its walls are covered with patterns and lines of poetry carved in plaster.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="212" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba</span><span class="kd-card-d">A forest of more than 800 columns holding up red-and-white striped arches.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/averroes"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/averroes.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="344" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Averroes</span><span class="kd-card-d">A thinker from Córdoba whose writings about Aristotle were read in Europe for centuries.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta.jpg" alt="" width="291" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ibn Battuta</span><span class="kd-card-d">A traveller from Morocco who journeyed for nearly 30 years across Africa and Asia.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi.jpg" alt="" width="315" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">al-Khwarizmi</span><span class="kd-card-d">A mathematician whose book about algebra was studied in Europe for hundreds of years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="216" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Dome of the Rock</span><span class="kd-card-d">A shrine in Jerusalem with a golden dome, finished around 691.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samarkand.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Samarkand</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Silk Road city famous for its blue-tiled mosques and schools.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isfahan.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="227" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Isfahan</span><span class="kd-card-d">A Persian city with a vast square that was once used for games of polo.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg.jpg" alt="" width="314" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ulugh Beg</span><span class="kd-card-d">A ruler of Samarkand who built an observatory and measured the length of the year very precisely.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-islamic">Play the The Islamic Golden Age Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-japan" aria-labelledby="t-japan-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-japan-h">Samurai and Old Japan</h2><p class="kd-intro">Warriors, castles and poets of the islands of the rising sun.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/samurai"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samurai.jpg" alt="" width="280" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Samurai</span><span class="kd-card-d">Japan's warriors, trained in archery, riding and the sword.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kyoto.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Kyoto</span><span class="kd-card-d">Japan's capital for more than a thousand years, full of temples and gardens.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/shinto"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shinto.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Shinto</span><span class="kd-card-d">Japan's oldest religion, which honours the kami, the spirits of nature.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="365" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Tokugawa Ieyasu</span><span class="kd-card-d">The shogun who founded a family that ruled Japan in peace for more than 250 years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ninja"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ninja.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="593" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ninja</span><span class="kd-card-d">Spies of old Japan who gathered secrets; many stories about them are legends.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/mount-fuji"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-fuji.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="156" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Mount Fuji</span><span class="kd-card-d">Japan's highest mountain, a volcano 3,776 metres high.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="350" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Oda Nobunaga</span><span class="kd-card-d">A warlord who began to unite Japan in the 1500s.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="320" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</span><span class="kd-card-d">He rose from a poor family to become the ruler of all Japan.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-japan">Play the Samurai and Old Japan Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section><section class="kd-sec" id="t-mesopotamia" aria-labelledby="t-mesopotamia-h"><h2 class="kd-h" id="t-mesopotamia-h">Ancient Mesopotamia</h2><p class="kd-intro">The land between two rivers, where the first cities and writing began.</p><ul class="kd-cards"><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hammurabi.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="353" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Hammurabi</span><span class="kd-card-d">A king of Babylon who had his laws carved on a tall stone for everyone to see.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat-of-ur"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ziggurat-of-ur.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="140" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ziggurat of Ur</span><span class="kd-card-d">Its builders used millions of mud bricks, some stamped with the king's name.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ishtar-gate"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ishtar-gate.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="199" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ishtar Gate</span><span class="kd-card-d">A gate of Babylon covered in blue glazed bricks with pictures of bulls and dragons.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/babylon"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babylon.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Babylon</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the greatest cities of the ancient world, on the banks of the Euphrates.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nineveh.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="219" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Nineveh</span><span class="kd-card-d">A great Assyrian city whose king kept a library of clay tablets.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal.jpg" alt="" width="315" height="420" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Ashurbanipal</span><span class="kd-card-d">An Assyrian king who collected thousands of clay tablets, among them the story of Gilgamesh.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="362" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Epic of Gilgamesh</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the oldest stories in the world, about a king who searched for everlasting life.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/standard-of-ur"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/standard-of-ur.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="240" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Standard of Ur</span><span class="kd-card-d">A box decorated with shell and blue stone, showing scenes of war and peace from about 4,500 years ago.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/tigris"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tigris.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Tigris</span><span class="kd-card-d">One of the two great rivers that gave Mesopotamia its name: the land between the rivers.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="kd-card" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates"><span class="kd-card-img"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euphrates.jpg" alt="" width="320" height="213" loading="lazy"></span><span class="kd-card-b"><span class="kd-card-t">Euphrates</span><span class="kd-card-d">The river beside which Babylon was built.</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="kd-more"><a class="kd-btn" href="/games#words-mesopotamia">Play the Ancient Mesopotamia Word Hunt &rarr;</a></p></section>
