<script>var KIDS_FACTS = [["Cleopatra lived closer in time to the first Moon landing than to the building of the Great Pyramid.","/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-giza"],["The Great Pyramid was built from more than two million blocks of stone.","/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-giza"],["The Great Wall of China cannot really be seen from the Moon with your own eyes.","/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china"],["Vikings did not wear horned helmets in battle: that idea comes from costumes and paintings of the 1800s.","/encyclopedia/vikings"],["The first Olympic Games, according to Greek tradition, were held in 776 BCE.","/encyclopedia/ancient-olympic-games"],["Paper was invented in China about 2,000 years ago.","/encyclopedia/papermaking"],["The Bayeux Tapestry is not really a tapestry at all: it is embroidered with wool on linen.","/encyclopedia/bayeux-tapestry"],["Napoleon was about 1.69 metres tall, ordinary for his time: the idea that he was very short is a myth.","/encyclopedia/napoleon"],["At Pompeii, loaves of bread were found still in an oven, buried by the eruption of 79 CE.","/encyclopedia/pompeii"],["Mansa Musa of Mali gave away so much gold on his way to Mecca in 1324 that its price fell in Cairo.","/encyclopedia/mansa-musa"],["The Hanging Gardens of Babylon are the only one of the Seven Wonders whose site has never been found.","/encyclopedia/hanging-gardens-of-babylon"],["Roman concrete was so strong that buildings made with it are still standing nearly 2,000 years later.","/empire/roman-empire"],["Writing was invented in Mesopotamia more than 5,000 years ago, with marks pressed into wet clay.","/encyclopedia/cuneiform"],["William Shakespeare is the first known writer to use hundreds of English words.","/encyclopedia/william-shakespeare"],["Queen Elizabeth I could speak French, Italian and Latin, and she studied Greek too.","/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i"],["Leonardo da Vinci wrote many of his notes backwards, in mirror writing.","/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci"],["Tutankhamun became pharaoh when he was only about nine years old.",""],["The Romans held a winter festival called Saturnalia, with feasts, games and presents.",""],["The Maya kept two calendars at once, one of 260 days and one of 365 days.",""],["Under Genghis Khan's sons and grandsons, the Mongol Empire became the largest land empire there has ever been.","/empire/mongol-empire"],["Marco Polo amazed Europeans by describing paper money, which he had seen in China.","/encyclopedia/marco-polo"],["The Eiffel Tower was built for a world's fair in 1889 and was only meant to stand for 20 years.",""],["Louis XIV of France reigned for 72 years, longer than any other European king.","/encyclopedia/louis-xiv"],["Isaac Newton also ran England's Royal Mint and hunted down people who made fake coins.","/encyclopedia/isaac-newton"],["Gutenberg printed his famous Bible in Mainz around 1455.","/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg"],["Hannibal crossed the Alps in 218 BCE with an army and about 37 elephants.","/encyclopedia/hannibal"],["The Silk Road was not one road but a web of trade routes stretching across Asia.","/encyclopedia/silk-road"],["Alexander the Great founded many cities called Alexandria; the most famous is in Egypt.","/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great"],["The Tower of London once kept a royal zoo, with lions and even a polar bear.",""],["The ancient Egyptians loved cats, and many cats were mummified.",""],["In Roman public toilets, people sat side by side on long stone benches.",""],["China's first emperor, Qin Shi Huang, was buried with an army of thousands of life-size clay soldiers.","/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang"],["Actors in ancient Greek plays wore big masks so that everyone in the theatre could tell who they were playing.",""],["The Aztec capital, Tenochtitlan, was built on an island in the middle of a lake.","/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan"],["Cyrus the Great of Persia is remembered for letting the peoples he conquered keep their own customs.","/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great"],["The Library of Alexandria may have held hundreds of thousands of scrolls.","/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria"],["Medieval castles had toilets called garderobes, which often emptied straight into the moat.",""],["At the pyramid of Chichén Itzá in Mexico, the evening sun at the equinox makes a shadow like a snake.",""],["Cleopatra came from a Greek family that ruled Egypt for nearly 300 years.","/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii"],["The Colosseum in Rome could hold at least 50,000 people.",""],["Roman towns had snack bars that sold hot food to people in the street.",""],["The oldest photograph that still survives was taken in France around 1826.",""],["The Wright brothers' first powered flight, in 1903, lasted just 12 seconds.",""],["The windows around the base of the Hagia Sophia's dome make it look as if it is floating.","/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia"],["The Inca had no alphabet, but they kept records with knotted strings called quipu.","/empire/inca-empire"],["Inca messengers ran in relays along mountain roads to carry news across the empire.","/empire/inca-empire"],["Queen Victoria was the first ruler of Britain to live in Buckingham Palace.","/encyclopedia/queen-victoria"],["Peter the Great of Russia was more than two metres tall.","/encyclopedia/peter-the-great"],["The ancient Egyptians made one of the first calendars with 365 days.",""],["Kites were invented in China more than 2,000 years ago.",""],["Chess began in India about 1,500 years ago, in a game called chaturanga.",""],["The Rosetta Stone is a piece of a bigger stone: its top and one corner are missing.","/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone"],["Ancient Greek runners at Olympia raced in a stadium about 192 metres long.","/encyclopedia/ancient-olympic-games"],["The Statue of Liberty was a gift from the people of France to the United States in 1886.",""],["Sparta's kings were always two at a time, from two different royal families.","/encyclopedia/sparta"],["The Vikings reached North America about a thousand years ago, at L'Anse aux Meadows in Canada.","/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows"],["Zheng He's fleet in 1405 had more than 200 ships and about 27,000 sailors and soldiers.","/encyclopedia/zheng-he"],["Ibn Battuta's travels covered about 120,000 kilometres, three times around the Earth.","/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta"],["The word \"algorithm\" comes from the name of the Baghdad scholar al-Khwarizmi.","/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi"],["In 1610 Galileo published drawings of the Moon's mountains and craters, seen through his own telescope.","/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei"],["Machu Picchu's finest walls were built without mortar: their stones are cut to fit together tightly.","/encyclopedia/machu-picchu"],["The Taj Mahal took about 20 years and 20,000 workers to build.","/encyclopedia/taj-mahal"],["The Chinese invented paper, printing, the magnetic compass and gunpowder, often called the Four Great Inventions.","/encyclopedia/papermaking"],["The Inca built a network of roads about 40,000 kilometres long across the Andes.","/encyclopedia/qhapaq-nan"],["Polynesian voyagers reached Hawaii, New Zealand and Easter Island long before Europeans sailed the Pacific.","/encyclopedia/polynesians"],["The Indian mathematician Brahmagupta wrote rules for calculating with zero in the year 628.",""],["Samurai armour was made of small plates laced together with cords of silk or leather.","/encyclopedia/samurai"],["The people of Mesopotamia counted in sixties, which is why an hour has 60 minutes.","/encyclopedia/sumer"],["Great Zimbabwe gave its name to the modern country of Zimbabwe.","/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe"],["The Aztecs used cacao beans as money.","/encyclopedia/cacao"]];</script>
